version: 4
metadata:
  name: ur5e-pick-place
  title: "UR5e pick-and-place: how accurate does the reported box pose have to be?"
  description: |
    A UR5e with a Robotiq 2F-85 is bolted to a workbench. A 60 mm box sits in front of it and an
    open bin stands to one side. Each trial: read the box's pose from a simulated detector, plan
    with MoveIt 2, pick the box up, carry it across and drop it in the bin. In roqsim (MuJoCo),
    over ROS 2.

    The factor is `position_stddev` -- the noise on the reported box pose. The jaws open to about
    87 mm around a 60 mm box, so there is ~13 mm of clearance PER SIDE, and that is the entire
    error budget for detection plus IK plus execution. The widest level is expected to fail, which
    is the point: an example that only ever passes shows nothing about where the edge is.
  keywords: [robotics, manipulation, pick-and-place, moveit2, mujoco, roqsim, ur5e, robotiq,
             grasping, perception-noise]

configuration:
# A configuration name may carry no underscores, spaces or periods (schema rule), hence `noise`
# -- the FACTOR keeps its full name.
- name: noise
  variations:
  # A `sim:` override, not a scenario parameter: the noise is a property of the WORLD, and the
  # roqsim backend mounts the campaign's overrides as a document and hands the simulator
  # `--override`. So the sweep needs no plumbing through the scenario at all.
  #
  # The path is `components.<entity>.<plugin>.<key>`. An override matching nothing is refused
  # outright, which is the good case -- the bad one is a path that is accepted and lands nowhere.
  #
  # 0.0 is the control: it isolates how much of a failure is the grasp and the arm rather than the
  # perception. Only the error component ACROSS the jaws spends the clearance, so the levels run
  # well past 13 mm -- at 28 mm per axis the across-jaw component exceeds the budget often enough
  # that failure is systematic rather than incidental.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      sim: components.ur5e.object_detector.position_stddev
      values: [0.0, 0.008, 0.016, 0.028]

execution:
  # The robot description MoveIt plans against, DERIVED from the world the simulator loads rather
  # than committed beside it: `roqsim export moveit` writes all six files move_group needs -- the
  # URDF and its meshes, the SRDF with a sampled collision matrix, and the four YAMLs. The outputs
  # are appended to `run_files` and content-hashed into the configuration's identity, and the step
  # is skipped when nothing it reads has changed, so a stale description cannot happen.
  #
  # `--root-link base`: the detector reports in the MuJoCo body `base`, and that string is also its
  # published `frame_id`; the UR arms have no `base_link` body at all. Exported under the default
  # name, detections and MoveIt's planning frame would name the same frame differently, so every
  # goal would need a transform that does not exist.
  #
  # `--tip-site pinch` ends the arm chain at the gripper's own grasp site instead of the flange, so
  # a goal for the fingertips is not an offset from the flange with every orientation tolerance
  # multiplied by that lever arm.
  #
  # `image:` is SYMBOLIC, so this names no registry: the deployment decides which project serves
  # `robovast-roqsim`, the image carrying both roqsim and the exporter.
  #
  # `inputs` names the whole `world/` directory rather than the world file alone, because the world
  # spawns a prop that lives beside it and the generator stages only what it is given. The
  # reference inside the world resolves against the world's own directory wherever it is staged, so
  # the same file compiles here and in the simulator.
  generate:
  - shell:
      out: moveit/gen
      image: family:robovast-roqsim
      inputs: ["world"]
      command: >-
        bash -lc "source /ws/install/setup.bash &&
        roqsim export moveit --world {inputs[0]}/ur5e_pick_place.yaml --out {out}
        --name ur5e_2f85 --root-link base --tip-site pinch
        --manifest {out}/.generated.json --check"

  containers:
  # The simulator, in a container of its own, running roqsim's ordinary CLI -- the same command
  # that debugs this world by hand.
    simulation:
      backend: roqsim
      config: world/ur5e_pick_place.yaml
      # Measured over a full sweep, with headroom left above the measurement. An example's
      # reservations get copied, so they should be the ones a reader ought to copy: over-reserving
      # costs nothing on an idle lane and a multiple of the throughput on a busy one, on every job
      # of every sweep.
      resources:
        cpu: 1
        memory: 1Gi

  # The system under test: move_group and robot_state_publisher.
  #
  # `family:robovast-roqsim` rather than the default member, because MoveIt's SERVERS ship in the
  # roqsim flavour -- what executes a MoveIt trajectory is roqsim's own bridge. SYMBOLIC, so it
  # still names no registry. Nothing in this file declares `system_packages`: the campaign builds
  # no image of its own, and the sut runs one the node has already pulled for the simulator.
    sut:
      image: family:robovast-roqsim
      # The most generous of the three: move_group's planning bursts are what this has to absorb.
      resources:
        cpu: 2
        memory: 1Gi

  # The trial and the measurement, nothing else. The DEFAULT image: `moveit_msgs` is in the base,
  # and a MoveGroup client needs the messages, not the servers.
    scenario:
      resources:
        cpu: 1
        memory: 512Mi

  mode: ros2
  # The outcome is a coin weighted by the noise draw, so the repetitions have to separate rates
  # rather than confirm an outcome: 3 cannot tell a 30% failure rate from a 60% one. 4 x 5 = 20
  # runs still finishes in minutes.
  runs: 5
  scenario_file: scenario.osc
  # Per-extension globs. NOT `files/*`: that pattern crosses `/` and sweeps `__pycache__/*.pyc`
  # into the configuration's identity hash, so stale bytecode changes what looks like the same
  # configuration.
  run_files:
  - "files/*.py"
  - "moveit/*.py"
  - "world/*.yaml"
  - "world/*.xml"
  # The generated description. The meshes are named separately because the URDF references them by
  # path: without them move_group loads a robot with NO collision geometry and plans straight
  # through the bench, which is a wrong answer rather than a crash.
  - "moveit/gen/*.urdf"
  - "moveit/gen/*.srdf"
  - "moveit/gen/*.yaml"
  - "moveit/gen/meshes/*.stl"
  # Generous, and it has to be: a trial is ~30 s of arm motion, but move_group takes the better
  # part of a minute to come up (planners, kinematics plugin, the planning scene).
  timeout: 900

# The box's own trajectory, so a failure can be READ rather than guessed at: `out.csv` says the box
# never rose, and this says where it was while the arm was busy not lifting it. `out.csv` itself
# needs no step here -- a CSV in the run directory is ingested as a table named after the file.
#
# `require:` makes a missing frame a loud failure instead of an empty table. The box is the frame
# the verdict is built from, so a run that recorded no box pose measured nothing at all.
results_processing:
  postprocessing:
  - rosbags_tf_to_csv:
      frames: all
      require: [graspable_box]

# Replay one run. Three processes have to be read together to see what happened -- the trial's
# phase, move_group's planner, and the simulator -- and the merged log is the only place they share
# a timeline. Without it a failure in any of them looks identical from outside: nothing moved.
visualization:
  results:
    run_view:
      panels:
      - scenario_tree:
          title: Trial
          position: {anchor: top-left, width: 320, height: "calc(50% - 44px)"}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: behaviors}
      - log:
          title: Trial, move_group and simulator
          position: {anchor: bottom, height: 240}
          minimizable: true
